.TH auto-set-conf-var 8

\" auto-man2man (used in generating subcommand lists) depends on this section
.SH NAME
.PP
auto-set-conf-var - Set a variable in a configuration file

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-set-conf-var variable value file caller
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
variable    Variable to add or change
value       New value of variable
file        Absolute path of the configuration file
caller      Name of calling script, often "$0"
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-set-conf-var
command adds or changes the value of a variable in a configuration
file such as /etc/sysctl.conf, /boot/loader.conf, or any other file
containing entries of the form "variable=value".

It provides a controlled and non-interactive alternative to manually
editing files, and a portable, file-agnostic alternative to
system-specific interfaces such as FreeBSD's sysrc(8).

If "variable" was not already in the configuration file, an entry is
added.
If caller is not "nocomment", the new entry is wrapped in a
comment as follows:

.nf
.na
# Added by auto-admin from $caller
var=value
# End $caller addition
.ad
.fi

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-set-conf-var acpi_video_load '"YES"' /boot/loader.conf $0
.ad
.fi

.SH FILES
.nf
.na
/etc/sysctl.conf, /boot/loader.conf
.ad
.fi

.SH SEE ALSO

auto-admin(8), auto-append-line(8)

